#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""[" -f "${BASH_SOURCE:-$0}" ] || { echo "a Python script: run it with python3" >&2; exit 126; }; exec python3 "${BASH_SOURCE:-$0}" "$@" || exit # sh/bash: hand off to python3 before reading the docstring
Is this skill installed on THIS machine, and is it current?

    python3 check              every platform in config.json platforms.enabled
    python3 check ios          one platform
    python3 check android

Prints ONE line and exits:

    0  installed, current      -> proceed
    1  installed, but stale    -> re-run INSTRUCTION.md, then re-check
       (or NOT FULLY MEASURED: no install context to read, or a leg such as
       the hypervisor that this machine's probe cannot measure - the line says
       NO VERDICT and names the leg)
    2  not installed           -> follow INSTRUCTION.md

With several platforms the exit is the WORST state found, and the line names
each platform's contribution.

STATE 1 IS THE REASON THIS EXISTS. Absent is loud: the tool is not there, the
build dies naming it, nothing runs. Stale is silent: every binary answers,
every command runs, and the behaviour is subtly the old one.

THE SPECIFIC FILES ARE READ FROM THE INSTALL CONTEXT, THROUGH fleetlib, NEVER
BESIDE THIS SCRIPT. config.json and installation/.install-state.json live in
fleetlib.paths.skill_context('device-emulators'); the fleet's skill directory
holds COMMON files only. The repository paths config names (build.script,
android.hypervisor_probe) resolve against the INSTALL ROOT, the directory holding
.claude/fleet.json, because they are the repository's own commands and an
operator runs them from its root. When no install context resolves, nothing was
looked at and the line says NO VERDICT. The fleet root comes from
realpath(__file__): through an agent's personal skill link, abspath would name
the link's directory. check.test.py beside this file drives the states below
against the fleet's fixture install; its header lists each case and the legs
that have none.

THIS FILE IS ALSO THE SKILL'S MACHINE INVENTORY, AND THE ONLY ONE. Nothing in
SKILL.md, technical/, config.json or CONTEXT.md may transcribe a version, an
SDK location, a runtime or an AVD: an install is worked from several machines
and those files are committed, so a written inventory is wrong everywhere but
where it was written while still reading as authoritative. It is computed
here, on demand, and it cannot go stale.

WHY THIS CHECKS REAL ARTIFACTS AND NOT A MARKER FILE. A marker records that
somebody once ran an install. It cannot tell you that Xcode is still selected,
that an iOS runtime survived an upgrade, or that an AVD was deleted in Device
Manager. Every test below therefore asks the machine, and only the LAST reads
.install-state.json. If you replace any of these with a flag in the state
file, the check keeps passing on a machine where nothing works. Do not.

WHY THE STATE FILE IS KEYED BY HOST. It is a SPECIFIC file, committed like
everything else, so it travels to machines that never ran the install. A
single `host` field would make every OTHER machine report stale, and
re-recording it would un-record the machine before it - so two machines could
never both be installed. `versions` is a hostname -> record map and each
machine records only itself. The key is `versions`, where other fleet checks
write `hosts`, because installs already hold per-host records under it and a
renamed key would report every one of those machines stale for a record that
is still true.

WHY A PLATFORM YOU DO NOT RUN BELONGS OUT OF platforms.enabled. Exiting on the
worst state means one absent SDK would otherwise report the whole skill as
uninstalled and block the platform that works. Enabling a platform is the
statement "this machine is supposed to run it".

WHY THE ANDROID SDK RESOLUTION ORDER BELOW MUST STAY IDENTICAL TO THE BUILD
SCRIPT'S. PATH is not the right oracle: adb and emulator reach a developer's
PATH from an interactive shell rc, and every non-interactive caller (CI, a git
hook, an agent's tool shell, cron) sees a PATH with no Android SDK on it. A
PATH-only check tells a reader with a complete SDK to go install one. If this
order drifts from the build script's, this check reports a state the build
script does not agree with, which is worse than not checking at all.

WHY THE ANDROID ARM PROBES THE HYPERVISOR, WHY THAT COMES BEFORE THE AVD LIST,
AND WHY THE PROBE ITSELF IS NOT IN THIS FILE. An AVD is a directory; a BOOTING
AVD needs a hypervisor under it, and both ways that can be missing are invisible
to every other test here - `emulator -list-avds` lists an AVD that cannot start
exactly as it lists one that can. A machine whose only fault is an unopenable
/dev/kvm would otherwise be reported as missing an AVD, and ordering the probe
BEFORE the AVD legs is the point rather than a preference: told "no AVD named
X", an operator creates X, and X does not boot either.

THE LEGS LIVE IN THE REPOSITORY SCRIPT NAMED BY config.json
android.hypervisor_probe, NOT HERE, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING. The build script
asks the identical question at a different moment - when an AVD has failed to
attach to adb, so that its die message can name the right remedy instead of
telling an operator with an unbootable AVD to raise a TIMEOUT. ONE
implementation, two thin consumers. The probe's interface is SKILL.md, "The two
scripts": check_hypervisor(<emulator path>) -> (ok, detail), where ok is True
(usable), False (a named cause) or None (NOT MEASURED on this machine). Do not
re-inline the legs here.

WHY A HYPERVISOR THE PROBE CANNOT MEASURE IS EXIT 1 AND NEVER A PASS. A probe
that does not measure a platform (macOS, where the accelerator is HVF and there
is no /dev/kvm) knows nothing about whether an AVD boots there, and exit 0
means proceed: reporting that as installed is the marker-file lie with the
probe's words printed beside it. So None is NO VERDICT, exit 1, naming the leg
and the probe's words, after every leg that CAN be measured has run. The cost
is real and stated: `check` exits on the worst platform, so a Mac running both
platforms reads 1 overall while its iOS leg is fine; it checks each platform
on its own (`check ios`, `check android`) and confirms the Android accelerator
by hand (`emulator -accel-check`). A probe that answers True with "not probed"
words is read as True, because ok is the probe's verdict and the check does not
parse its prose; SKILL.md tells a probe to answer None where it cannot measure.

WHY THE iOS REMOTE ARM RESOLVES THE HOSTNAME BEFORE IT OPENS AN ssh. Every other
failure on that arm has a remediation about keys, host keys or Xcode, and ssh
reports an unresolvable hostname in words that read as a key problem - which
sends the operator to fix something that is not broken. Resolution is cheap on
the happy path, and its fix (bring up the mesh that publishes the name, or
override IOS_REMOTE_HOST) shares nothing with the others. It resolves what ssh
would resolve, not the configured string: an ssh_config Host alias
legitimately points somewhere else, and a ProxyJump, a ProxyCommand or a LIVE
ControlMaster socket each mean the name is not resolved here at all, so this
leg stands down rather than reporting a false red.
getaddrinfo takes NO timeout and setdefaulttimeout does not reach it, so
against a blackholing nameserver it blocks for the resolver's whole retry
budget - tens of seconds, where every other outbound call in this file is bounded
by run(..., timeout=). It therefore runs on a thread this function abandons after
RESOLVE_TIMEOUT, and a lookup that does not answer in time is reported AS one,
never as a name that does not resolve: those have different fixes and a check
that cannot tell them apart sends the operator to the wrong one. It also catches
more than gaierror - a label over 63 bytes raises UnicodeError from the idna
codec, and IOS_REMOTE_HOST is precisely the operator-typed input that produces
one - because an escaping exception here exits 1, which means "STALE install
record, re-run INSTRUCTION.md" and cannot fix a malformed hostname. Do not
simplify either property away.

WHY THE iOS RUNTIME AND THE DEVICE ARE TWO SEPARATE TESTS. A fresh CI agent
routinely has device TYPES and no runtime, and `simctl list devices` still
prints devices for a runtime it cannot boot. "Available" is the only word that
means bootable, and the two failures need different fixes:
`xcodebuild -downloadPlatform iOS` against `xcrun simctl create`.
"""
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import shutil
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import threading

HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
SKILL = os.path.dirname(HERE)
FLEET_ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(SKILL)))
SKILL_NAME = "device-emulators"

# Set by main() from the install context; read by the probes below.
REPO = None
STATE = None

# The build script must resolve the SDK the same way. See the header. After
# these, the NEWEST build-tools/<version>/ (where aapt2 lives): the lexically
# last match, which is version order for every build-tools release.
SDK_SUBDIRS = ("platform-tools", "emulator", "cmdline-tools/latest/bin")

KNOWN_PLATFORMS = ("ios", "android")


def out(code, msg):
    print(msg)
    sys.exit(code)


def declared_version():
    """The version SKILL.md declares. Absent means the skill predates the
    convention, which is itself a reason to re-install rather than to guess."""
    with open(os.path.join(SKILL, "SKILL.md"), encoding="utf-8") as f:
        head = f.read(4096)
    m = re.search(r"^version:\s*(\d+)\s*$", head, re.M)
    return int(m.group(1)) if m else 0


def run(cmd, timeout=180):
    try:
        return subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout)
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - a tool that will not run is one we do not have
        return None


def dig(cfg, path):
    node = cfg
    for part in path.split("."):
        node = node.get(part) if isinstance(node, dict) else None
    return node


def expand(value, local=True):
    """Expand a config value with fleetlib's expander: ${VAR:-default}, $VAR /
    ${VAR} and a leading ~. An unset $VAR with no default stays literal, so a
    command that uses it fails naming the variable nobody set.

    local=False FOR ANY PATH THAT LIVES ON ANOTHER MACHINE. `~` and `$HOME`
    mean THIS machine's home to fleetlib, and a remote workdir written `~/x`
    would be resolved against the local home and then tested on the remote,
    where that path does not exist - reported as "the remote is not set up",
    which is a false and expensive diagnosis. So HOME is handed to the expander
    as a bare `~`: both `~` and `$HOME` then come out as `~`, which the REMOTE
    shell expands against the REMOTE home."""
    if not isinstance(value, str):
        return value
    from fleetlib import expand as fx
    if local:
        return fx.value(value)
    return fx.value(value, env=dict(os.environ, HOME="~"))


def repo_path(value):
    """A config value that names a file in the install's repository, as that
    file's path: expanded, and a relative result joined to the install root
    (/claude-skill section 2: repository paths resolve against the install root,
    every other relative path against config.json's directory). The root is
    taken through realpath before the join, as fleetlib resolves a config path.

    This is fleetlib.paths.resolve_repo_path's job; it lives here until that
    helper is on this branch, and then this function becomes a call to it."""
    return os.path.normpath(os.path.join(os.path.realpath(REPO), expand(value)))


def android_sdk_tool(name):
    """Absolute path of an Android SDK binary, or None. See the header for why
    PATH alone is the wrong oracle. Returns a path, never a bare name, so no
    later call can silently pick a different binary."""
    found = shutil.which(name)
    if found:
        return found
    home = os.path.expanduser("~")
    roots = [
        os.environ.get("ANDROID_HOME", ""),
        os.environ.get("ANDROID_SDK_ROOT", ""),
        os.path.join(home, "Library/Android/sdk"),   # macOS default
        os.path.join(home, "Android/Sdk"),           # Linux default
    ]
    for root in roots:
        if not root:
            continue
        for sub in SDK_SUBDIRS:
            p = os.path.join(root, sub, name)
            if os.path.isfile(p) and os.access(p, os.X_OK):
                return p
        bt = os.path.join(root, "build-tools")
        hits = sorted(os.path.join(bt, v, name) for v in (os.listdir(bt) if os.path.isdir(bt) else []))
        hits = [p for p in hits if os.path.isfile(p) and os.access(p, os.X_OK)]
        if hits:
            return hits[-1]
    return None


def java_major(java):
    """Major version `java -version` reports, or None if it cannot be read.
    Both `1.8.0_x` and `17.0.1` shapes are handled; the output is on stderr."""
    r = run([java, "-version"], timeout=60)
    if not r:
        return None
    m = re.search(r'version "(\d+)(?:\.(\d+))?', (r.stderr or "") + (r.stdout or ""))
    if not m:
        return None
    major = int(m.group(1))
    return int(m.group(2) or 0) if major == 1 else major


# The bound on the iOS hostname lookup. See the header: getaddrinfo cannot be
# given a timeout, so this is enforced by abandoning the thread it runs on.
RESOLVE_TIMEOUT = 10


def load_hypervisor_probe(cfg):
    """Import the ONE hypervisor implementation -> (module, error message).

    The legs, the remediation wording and the three-state verdict vocabulary
    live in ONE repository file that both consumers load - this check and the
    build script - and this one is thin. DO NOT RE-INLINE THEM HERE "to keep
    the check self-contained": the copy that gets fixed and the copy that does
    not is how a fixed bug comes back, and neither author is present when it
    does.

    RESOLVED FROM config.json (android.hypervisor_probe) against the install
    root, NOT HARDCODED, because an adopting repository puts its scripts
    somewhere else. Same shape as build.script and build.env_config_script.

    A MISSING PROBE IS A REPORTED FAILURE, NEVER A SKIPPED LEG. A leg that
    silently stands down because its implementation is absent is the "skip
    indistinguishable from a pass" defect: the check would go green on a machine
    whose AVDs cannot boot, which is the one state this leg exists to catch."""
    rel = dig(cfg, "android.hypervisor_probe")
    if not rel:
        return None, ("android: config.json is missing required key "
                      "android.hypervisor_probe, so the hypervisor - the one thing that "
                      "decides whether ANY AVD here can boot - would go unmeasured")
    path = repo_path(rel)
    try:
        import importlib.util as _ilu
        spec = _ilu.spec_from_file_location("android_hypervisor_probe", path)
        if spec is None or spec.loader is None:
            raise ImportError(f"no loader for {path}")
        mod = _ilu.module_from_spec(spec)
        spec.loader.exec_module(mod)
        if not callable(getattr(mod, "check_hypervisor", None)):
            raise ImportError("it defines no check_hypervisor(emulator_path)")
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 - any failure to load is a leg we cannot run
        return None, (f"android: the hypervisor probe named by config.json "
                      f"android.hypervisor_probe is not loadable at {path} "
                      f"({type(e).__name__}: {e}), so whether any AVD on this machine can "
                      f"boot is UNMEASURED. Restore it from the repository.")
    return mod, None


# ---- per-platform probes: each returns (ok, message) ------------------------

def check_ios_remote(cfg):
    """The SSH prerequisite for the remote route, in ONE round trip.

    WHAT THIS PROVES, AND IT IS DELIBERATELY LESS THAN "the remote loop works":
    that `ssh <user>@<host>` authenticates NON-INTERACTIVELY (BatchMode refuses
    to fall back to a password prompt, which is the failure that otherwise hangs
    every command in the loop with nobody present to type into it), and that the
    Mac on the other end has a selected Xcode and at least one bootable iPhone.

    WHAT IT DOES NOT PROVE, STATED IN ITS OWN OUTPUT LINE SO NOBODY READS IT AS
    MORE: that the remote can AUTHENTICATE THE BUILD. The build CLI's credential
    may legitimately be supplied per-invocation through the SSH environment, in
    which case there is nothing on the remote's disk to measure and a probe here
    would be measuring the wrong machine at the wrong moment. That leg is proved
    by hand, once, in the Setup subsection of technical/ios.md - which is why
    this line names what it covered instead of saying `ios: ok`.

    A one-line remote probe rather than several: each `ssh` is a full handshake,
    and a check that takes ten seconds gets skipped."""
    host = expand(dig(cfg, "ios.remote.host"))
    user = expand(dig(cfg, "ios.remote.user"))
    # local=False: this path is on the OTHER machine (see expand's docstring).
    workdir = expand(dig(cfg, "ios.remote.workdir"), local=False) or ""
    if not user:
        return False, ("ios: config.json sets ios.remote.host but not ios.remote.user; "
                       "both are needed to form the ssh target")
    # The device-type keys are used in this function's own REMEDIATION line. Assert
    # them here rather than relying on the caller: an absent key would otherwise
    # tell the operator to run `simctl create 'None' 'None'`, which is a wrong
    # instruction dressed as a helpful one.
    for path in ("ios.device_type", "ios.device_type_id"):
        if not dig(cfg, path):
            return False, f"ios: config.json is missing required key {path}"
    target = f"{user}@{host}"

    # FIRST, and locally: does the name resolve at all? See the header for why
    # this cannot be left to ssh. Resolve what SSH would resolve, not the
    # configured string - a Host alias legitimately maps elsewhere - and stand
    # down entirely when a proxy is configured, because the name is then
    # resolved on the far side and a local lookup would be a false red.
    resolved, bypass = host, ""
    control_path = ""
    g = run(["ssh", "-G", target], timeout=20)
    if g and g.returncode == 0:
        for line in (g.stdout or "").splitlines():
            key, _, val = line.partition(" ")
            val = val.strip()
            if key == "hostname" and val:
                resolved = val
            elif key in ("proxyjump", "proxycommand") and val and val.lower() != "none":
                bypass = key
            elif key == "controlpath" and val and val.lower() != "none":
                control_path = val
    # A LIVE ControlMaster socket carries the session over an already-open
    # connection, so ssh never resolves the name and a lookup here would red a
    # Mac that is perfectly reachable. The socket must EXIST, not merely be
    # configured: `controlmaster auto` with no live master still resolves.
    if not bypass and control_path and os.path.exists(control_path):
        bypass = "controlmaster"
    if not bypass:
        outcome = {}

        def _resolve():
            try:
                socket.getaddrinfo(resolved, 22, proto=socket.IPPROTO_TCP)
                outcome["ok"] = True
            except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 - see the header: gaierror is not the
                outcome["err"] = exc  # only way this fails, and an escape exits 1.
        t = threading.Thread(target=_resolve, daemon=True)
        t.start()
        t.join(RESOLVE_TIMEOUT)
        via = "" if resolved == host else f" (ssh_config maps {host} to it)"
        fix = (f"bring up the mesh or VPN that publishes the name, or add it to /etc/hosts, "
               f"or point this machine at the Mac without editing the committed config - "
               f"export IOS_REMOTE_HOST=<hostname-or-ip> (config.json ios.remote.host).")
        if t.is_alive():
            # Distinct from "does not resolve", deliberately: a resolver that is
            # not answering is a nameserver problem and the fix above is the
            # wrong one.
            return False, (
                f"ios: resolving the remote Mac's hostname '{resolved}'{via} did not answer "
                f"within {RESOLVE_TIMEOUT}s, so this check cannot say whether the remote "
                f"route is usable. That is a RESOLVER problem, not a missing name: a machine "
                f"ADMIN checks /etc/resolv.conf and the reachability of the nameservers "
                f"listed there.")
        if "err" in outcome:
            exc = outcome["err"]
            detail = getattr(exc, "strerror", None) or str(exc)
            return False, (
                f"ios: the remote Mac's hostname '{resolved}'{via} does not resolve on this "
                f"machine ({type(exc).__name__}: {detail}), so nothing on the remote route "
                f"can run. This is not a key or a host-key problem and ssh-copy-id will not "
                f"fix it: {fix}")
    # BatchMode=yes is the load-bearing flag: without it a host that has lost its
    # key falls through to a password prompt and this check BLOCKS instead of
    # failing, which reads as a hung machine rather than a missing key.
    # NO StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new. The setup step this probes is stated as
    # "key auth AND the Mac in known_hosts", and accept-new would SATISFY the second
    # half on first contact instead of MEASURING it - a check that quietly creates
    # the state it claims to verify. ssh-copy-id, which the install step already
    # requires, populates known_hosts, so a host that fails here genuinely has not
    # been set up.
    remote_cmd = ("xcode-select -p && xcodebuild -version && "
                  "xcrun simctl list -j devices available")
    if workdir:
        # Quote the path but leave a leading ~ OUTSIDE the quotes, so the REMOTE
        # shell expands it against the REMOTE home. Tested LAST and with its own
        # exit code, because "cannot reach the Mac" and "the Mac is fine but the
        # directory is missing" are different problems with different fixes, and
        # a check that cannot tell them apart sends the operator to the wrong one.
        quoted = ("~/" + shlex.quote(workdir[2:])) if workdir.startswith("~/") \
            else shlex.quote(workdir)
        remote_cmd += f" && {{ test -d {quoted} || {{ echo NO_WORKDIR; exit 9; }}; }}"
    r = run(["ssh", "-o", "BatchMode=yes", "-o", "ConnectTimeout=10", target, remote_cmd],
            timeout=90)
    if r is None:
        return False, f"ios: no usable ssh client on this host (needed to reach {target})"
    if r.returncode != 0:
        detail = ((r.stderr or r.stdout or "").strip().splitlines() or ["no output"])[-1]
        if r.returncode == 9:
            return False, (f"ios: {target} is reachable and its Xcode is fine, but "
                           f"ios.remote.workdir ({workdir}) does not exist there. "
                           f"Create it: ssh {target} 'mkdir -p {workdir}' - and keep it "
                           f"outside every CI runner's work tree.")
        return False, (f"ios: cannot reach the remote Mac {target} non-interactively "
                       f"({detail}). Install a key there and accept its host key "
                       f"(ssh-copy-id {target}), then confirm "
                       f"`ssh -o BatchMode=yes {target} true` exits 0.")
    outp = r.stdout or ""
    m = re.search(r"Xcode (\d+)", outp)
    xcode_major = int(m.group(1)) if m else 0
    min_major = int(dig(cfg, "ios.xcode_min_major") or 16)
    if xcode_major < min_major:
        return False, (f"ios: the remote Mac {target} has Xcode {xcode_major or '?'}, older "
                       f"than the required {min_major} (config.json ios.xcode_min_major)")
    brace = outp.find("{")
    try:
        devices = [d for group in json.loads(outp[brace:]).get("devices", {}).values()
                   for d in group
                   if d.get("isAvailable") and "iPhone" in (d.get("name") or "")]
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
        return False, (f"ios: the remote device list did not parse "
                       f"({type(e).__name__}: {e}); ran on {target}")
    if not devices:
        return False, (f"ios: no available iPhone simulator on {target}. Create one there: "
                       f"xcrun simctl create '{dig(cfg, 'ios.device_type')}' "
                       f"'{dig(cfg, 'ios.device_type_id')}'")
    return True, (f"ios: no local Simulator on {sys.platform}; REMOTE route to {target} - "
                  f"ssh key auth ok, {workdir or 'no workdir configured'} present, "
                  f"Xcode {xcode_major}, {len(devices)} iPhone device(s) there "
                  f"(the remote's BUILD credential is NOT covered by this check)")


def check_ios(cfg):
    if sys.platform != "darwin":
        if expand(dig(cfg, "ios.remote.host")):
            return check_ios_remote(cfg)
        return False, (f"ios: the iOS Simulator exists only on macOS and this host is "
                       f"{sys.platform}. Drop 'ios' from platforms.enabled here, or set "
                       f"ios.remote.host to a Mac this machine can drive over SSH.")

    for path in ("ios.sim_artifact", "ios.device_type", "ios.device_type_id"):
        if not dig(cfg, path):
            return False, f"ios: config.json is missing required key {path}"
    min_major = int(dig(cfg, "ios.xcode_min_major") or 16)

    if not shutil.which("xcrun"):
        return False, "ios: no xcrun on PATH. Install Xcode and the command line tools."
    r = run(["xcode-select", "-p"])
    devdir = (r.stdout or "").strip() if r and r.returncode == 0 else ""
    if not devdir or not os.path.isdir(devdir):
        return False, ("ios: no selected developer directory (xcode-select -p). Run: "
                       "sudo xcode-select -s /Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer")
    r = run(["xcodebuild", "-version"])
    if not r or r.returncode != 0:
        detail = ((r.stderr or r.stdout or "").strip().splitlines() or [""])[0] if r else "did not run"
        return False, (f"ios: xcodebuild -version failed ({detail}). Most often an "
                       "unaccepted licence: sudo xcodebuild -license accept, then "
                       "sudo xcodebuild -runFirstLaunch")
    m = re.search(r"Xcode (\d+)", r.stdout or "")
    xcode_major = int(m.group(1)) if m else 0
    if xcode_major < min_major:
        return False, (f"ios: Xcode {xcode_major or '?'} is older than the required "
                       f"{min_major} (config.json ios.xcode_min_major)")

    r = run(["xcrun", "simctl", "list", "-j", "runtimes"])
    if not r or r.returncode != 0:
        return False, ("ios: xcrun simctl list runtimes failed; the CoreSimulator service "
                       "is not usable on this machine")
    try:
        runtimes = [rt for rt in json.loads(r.stdout).get("runtimes", [])
                    if rt.get("isAvailable") and "iOS" in (rt.get("name") or "")]
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
        return False, f"ios: simctl runtime list did not parse ({type(e).__name__}: {e})"
    if not runtimes:
        return False, "ios: no available iOS runtime. Run: xcodebuild -downloadPlatform iOS"

    r = run(["xcrun", "simctl", "list", "-j", "devices", "available"])
    if not r or r.returncode != 0:
        return False, "ios: xcrun simctl list devices failed"
    try:
        devices = [d for group in json.loads(r.stdout).get("devices", {}).values()
                   for d in group
                   if d.get("isAvailable") and "iPhone" in (d.get("name") or "")]
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
        return False, f"ios: simctl device list did not parse ({type(e).__name__}: {e})"
    if not devices:
        return False, ("ios: no available iPhone simulator. Create one: xcrun simctl create "
                       f"'{dig(cfg, 'ios.device_type')}' '{dig(cfg, 'ios.device_type_id')}'")

    if not shutil.which("pod"):
        return False, ("ios: no CocoaPods on PATH (the prebuild runs pod install). Install "
                       "it with: brew install cocoapods")

    return True, (f"ios: Xcode {xcode_major}, {len(runtimes)} runtime(s), "
                  f"{len(devices)} iPhone device(s)")


def check_android(cfg):
    if not dig(cfg, "android.avd.default"):
        return False, "android: config.json is missing required key android.avd.default"
    avd_want = dig(cfg, "android.avd.default")
    avd_compat = dig(cfg, "android.avd.compat") or ""
    min_major = int(dig(cfg, "android.jdk_min_major") or 17)

    adb = android_sdk_tool("adb")
    if not adb:
        return False, ("android: adb not found on PATH, in $ANDROID_HOME, $ANDROID_SDK_ROOT, "
                       "~/Library/Android/sdk or ~/Android/Sdk. Install the SDK platform-tools.")
    emu = android_sdk_tool("emulator")
    if not emu:
        return False, ("android: emulator not found on PATH, in $ANDROID_HOME, "
                       "$ANDROID_SDK_ROOT, ~/Library/Android/sdk or ~/Android/Sdk. Install "
                       "the SDK emulator package.")
    # The build script reads every APK's application id with aapt2 before it
    # installs it, and refuses the install when it cannot.
    if not android_sdk_tool("aapt2"):
        return False, ("android: aapt2 not found on PATH or in any SDK's build-tools/ "
                       "($ANDROID_HOME, $ANDROID_SDK_ROOT, ~/Library/Android/sdk, ~/Android/Sdk). "
                       "Install an SDK build-tools package: the build script reads each APK's "
                       "application id with it before installing.")

    # BEFORE the JDK and the AVD legs: a machine with no hypervisor cannot boot
    # ANY AVD, so reporting a missing AVD there sends the operator to create one
    # that will not start either. See the header.
    probe, why = load_hypervisor_probe(cfg)
    if probe is None:
        return False, why
    try:
        hv_ok, hv = probe.check_hypervisor(emu)
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 - a probe that raises measured nothing
        return False, (f"android: the hypervisor probe raised {type(e).__name__}: {e}, so "
                       f"whether any AVD on this machine can boot is UNMEASURED")
    if hv_ok is False:
        return False, hv
    if hv_ok is not True and hv_ok is not None:
        return False, (f"android: the hypervisor probe answered {hv_ok!r}, which is none of "
                       f"True, False or None (SKILL.md, 'The two scripts'), so whether any AVD "
                       f"on this machine can boot is UNMEASURED")
    # hv_ok None: NOT MEASURED here. Every other leg still runs, so the line
    # that reports it also says whether the rest of the platform is ready.

    # THE SUBJECT IS `javac`, NOT `java`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE WHOLE
    # POINT OF THIS LEG. Gradle needs a JDK; a JRE is not one. A machine whose
    # only runtime is a JRE at or above the floor answers `java -version`
    # perfectly and cannot compile a line - and this check gating on `java`
    # would call it READY while the build script refuses it, which is strictly
    # worse than no check: the two would disagree about the same machine and
    # only one of them would be right. The build script qualifies every
    # candidate on `bin/javac` (SKILL.md, "The two scripts"); asking the same
    # question here is what keeps "a machine check calls ready is one the build
    # accepts" a true sentence rather than a hopeful one.
    #
    # The version is still read from `java`, because `javac -version` and
    # `java -version` differ in FORMAT and this file has one parser. They are
    # read from the SAME home, so they cannot disagree about the major.
    javac = shutil.which("javac")
    if not javac:
        jh = os.environ.get("JAVA_HOME", "")
        cand = os.path.join(jh, "bin", "javac") if jh else ""
        javac = cand if cand and os.access(cand, os.X_OK) else None
    if not javac:
        return False, (f"android: no javac on PATH or under $JAVA_HOME. A JRE is not enough - "
                       f"the Gradle build needs a JDK {min_major} or newer, and a machine whose "
                       f"only runtime is a JRE passes every 'is there a java?' check and still "
                       f"cannot compile.")
    java = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(javac), "java")
    if not os.access(java, os.X_OK):
        java = shutil.which("java")
    if not java:
        return False, f"android: {javac} has no java beside it, so the JDK version cannot be read"
    have_major = java_major(java)
    if have_major is None:
        return False, f"android: {java} does not report a version, so the JDK cannot be checked"
    if have_major < min_major:
        return False, (f"android: JDK {have_major} at {java} is older than the required "
                       f"{min_major} (config.json android.jdk_min_major)")

    # `emulator -list-avds` is deliberate: it needs no JDK, so on a machine with
    # the SDK and no JDK this still answers, where the Java avdmanager route
    # would turn "the AVD is missing" into a JAVA_HOME stack trace.
    r = run([emu, "-list-avds"])
    if not r or r.returncode != 0:
        return False, f"android: {emu} -list-avds did not run"
    avds = [ln.strip() for ln in (r.stdout or "").splitlines() if ln.strip()]
    if avd_want not in avds:
        return False, (f"android: no AVD named {avd_want} (config.json android.avd.default); "
                       f"-list-avds has {avds or 'none'}. Create it in Android Studio's "
                       f"Device Manager, or with avdmanager on a CI agent.")
    if avd_compat and avd_compat not in avds:
        return False, (f"android: no AVD named {avd_compat} (config.json android.avd.compat); "
                       f"-list-avds has {avds}. Create it, or clear android.avd.compat if this "
                       f"machine does not run the compat pass.")

    avd_words = f"AVDs {avd_want}" + (f" + {avd_compat}" if avd_compat else "")
    if hv_ok is None:
        return None, (f"android: the hypervisor was NOT MEASURED by the probe ({hv}); "
                      f"JDK {have_major} and {avd_words} are installed. Confirm by hand that "
                      f"`{emu} -accel-check` exits 0; on a machine that runs both platforms, "
                      f"check each on its own (check ios, check android)")
    return True, f"android: JDK {have_major}, accel [{hv}], {avd_words}"



PROBES = {"ios": check_ios, "android": check_android}


def main():
    global REPO, STATE
    # A readiness check writes nothing: no __pycache__ in the fleet tree (fleetlib)
    # or beside the repository's probe. First, before any import below.
    sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
    want = declared_version()

    # ---- the install context -------------------------------------------------
    sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(FLEET_ROOT, "lib"))
    try:
        from fleetlib import paths
        context = str(paths.skill_context(SKILL_NAME))
        REPO = str(paths.install_root())
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 - every fleetlib refusal is one verdict
        out(1, f"NO VERDICT: no install context for this skill ({type(e).__name__}: {e}). "
               f"Nothing was looked at, so this says nothing about this machine.")
    cfg_path = os.path.join(context, "config.json")
    STATE = os.path.join(context, "installation", ".install-state.json")

    # ---- config -------------------------------------------------------------
    if not os.path.exists(cfg_path):
        out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: no {cfg_path}. Run: `install -m 644 "
               f"{os.path.join(SKILL, 'config.example.json')} {cfg_path}`, then fill it in.")
    try:
        with open(cfg_path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            cfg = json.load(f)
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
        out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: {cfg_path} does not parse ({type(e).__name__}: {e})")
    if not isinstance(cfg, dict):
        out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: {cfg_path} is not a JSON object")

    for path in ("project.name", "build.script", "platforms.enabled"):
        if not dig(cfg, path):
            out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: {cfg_path} is missing required key {path}")

    # ---- which platforms -----------------------------------------------------
    arg = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else ""
    if arg in ("-h", "--help"):
        out(0, "usage: check [ios|android]   (default: every platform in platforms.enabled)")
    enabled = [p for p in dig(cfg, "platforms.enabled") if p in KNOWN_PLATFORMS]
    unknown = [p for p in dig(cfg, "platforms.enabled") if p not in KNOWN_PLATFORMS]
    if unknown:
        out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: config.json platforms.enabled names unknown platform(s) "
               f"{unknown}; this skill knows {list(KNOWN_PLATFORMS)}")
    if arg:
        if arg not in KNOWN_PLATFORMS:
            out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: unknown platform '{arg}'; want one of {list(KNOWN_PLATFORMS)}")
        if arg not in enabled:
            out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: '{arg}' is not in config.json platforms.enabled "
                   f"({enabled}). Add it there if this machine is supposed to run it.")
        platforms = [arg]
    else:
        platforms = enabled
    if not platforms:
        out(2, "NOT INSTALLED: config.json platforms.enabled is empty; nothing to check")

    # ---- the build script this skill is the operator's route through ---------
    script = repo_path(cfg["build"]["script"])
    if not (os.path.isfile(script) and os.access(script, os.X_OK)):
        out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: build.script is not an executable file at {script} "
               f"(resolved against the install root {REPO})")

    # ---- the build CLI's session, ON THE MACHINE THAT BUILDS -----------------
    # The local build declares a logged-in context, so an unauthenticated agent
    # fails at the BUILD rather than at a login step. Either route counts: the
    # token in the variable ci.eas_token_env names, or a session the CLI stored
    # in the home directory (~/.expo/state.json carrying `auth`).
    #
    # ASK IT OF THE MACHINE THAT ACTUALLY BUILDS, WHICH IS NOT ALWAYS THIS
    # ONE. With the remote iOS route (ios.remote.host on a non-macOS host) the
    # build runs on the Mac, so a session HERE proves nothing about it and the
    # ABSENCE of one here proves nothing either - demanding it would fail a
    # correctly-installed machine for a credential it does not use, which is the
    # worst kind of check: one that is red for a reason unrelated to the thing
    # it names. Android always builds locally, and iOS builds locally on macOS;
    # if neither of those is in play, this leg has no subject on this machine
    # and is skipped. The remote's own credential is section 4a.1 step 5 of
    # technical/ios.md, proved by hand, and check_ios_remote's output line says
    # in words that it is not covered here.
    builds_locally = any(p == "android" for p in platforms) or (
        "ios" in platforms and sys.platform == "darwin")
    token_env = dig(cfg, "ci.eas_token_env") or "EXPO_TOKEN"
    if builds_locally:
        session = os.path.expanduser("~/.expo/state.json")
        has_session = False
        if os.path.exists(session):
            try:
                with open(session, encoding="utf-8") as f:
                    has_session = bool(json.load(f).get("auth"))
            except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - an unreadable session is no session
                has_session = False
        if not (os.environ.get(token_env) or has_session):
            out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: the build CLI has no session and ${token_env} is unset. "
                   f"Export ${token_env} (CI) or log the CLI in (workstation).")

    # ---- per platform --------------------------------------------------------
    results = [(p,) + PROBES[p](cfg) for p in platforms]
    failed = [msg for _, ok, msg in results if ok is False]
    if failed:
        out(2, "NOT INSTALLED: " + " | ".join(failed))
    unmeasured = [msg for _, ok, msg in results if ok is None]
    inventory = ", ".join(msg for _, ok, msg in results if ok is True)

    # ---- OPTIONAL: the device-e2e tap driver --------------------------------
    # Reported, never enforced. The driver is needed by the e2e suite and never
    # by the operator loop, so a machine that only runs dev builds is fully
    # installed without it - failing here would be the same mistake as leaving
    # a platform enabled that this machine does not run.
    #
    # driver_path BEFORE PATH, deliberately: the installer appends its bin dir
    # to shell rc files, which are read by INTERACTIVE shells only, so every
    # non-interactive caller has a working install and no driver on PATH.
    de = cfg.get("device_e2e") or {}
    if de.get("driver"):
        # Not a repository path: expanded, and a relative value resolves against
        # config.json's directory (/claude-skill section 2).
        cand = (str(paths.resolve_config_path(de["driver_path"], cfg_path))
                if de.get("driver_path") else "")
        drv = cand if cand and os.access(cand, os.X_OK) else shutil.which(de["driver"])
        if drv:
            r = run([drv, "--version"], timeout=120)
            ver = ((r.stdout or "").strip().splitlines() or [""])[-1].strip() if r else ""
            inventory += f", device-e2e: {de['driver']} {ver or 'present'} ({drv})"
        else:
            inventory += f", device-e2e: no {de['driver']} (optional; the dev loop does not need it)"
        inventory = inventory.lstrip(", ")

    # ---- artifacts are all present. Only the recorded version can be wrong. --
    host = socket.gethostname()
    if not os.path.exists(STATE):
        out(1, f"STALE: installed, but no {STATE} records which version "
               f"(SKILL.md declares {want}). Re-run installation/INSTRUCTION.md.")
    try:
        with open(STATE, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            state = json.load(f)
        versions = state.get("versions") if isinstance(state, dict) else None
        if not isinstance(versions, dict):
            raise ValueError("no `versions` map")
        record = versions.get(host)
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
        out(1, f"STALE: {STATE} unreadable ({type(e).__name__}: {e}). Redo the record "
               f"step in installation/INSTRUCTION.md.")
    if not isinstance(record, dict):
        known = sorted(versions.keys())
        out(1, f"STALE: no install recorded for this machine ({host}); recorded hosts are "
               f"{known or 'none'}. The state file travels with the install; the machine "
               f"setup does not. Re-run installation/INSTRUCTION.md.")
    try:
        have = int(record.get("version", -1))
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
        have = -1
    if have < want:
        out(1, f"STALE: {host} recorded version {have}, SKILL.md declares {want}. "
               f"Re-run installation/INSTRUCTION.md.")
    if have > want:
        out(1, f"STALE: {host} recorded version {have}, NEWER than SKILL.md's {want}. "
               f"The fleet checkout is older than the one that recorded the install; "
               f"check `fleet status`.")

    # NOT FULLY MEASURED: every measurable artifact is present and the record is
    # current, but a leg the probe cannot measure says nothing either way. See
    # the header: exit 1, never 0, and the line names the leg.
    if unmeasured:
        out(1, f"NO VERDICT: installed and current (version {want}) on {host} for every "
               f"leg that was measured, but " + " | ".join(unmeasured)
               + (f". Measured: {inventory}" if inventory else ""))

    out(0, f"OK: installed and current (version {want}) on {host} - {inventory}")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
